<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salzwedel</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salzwedel is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altmark">Altmark</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the Jeetze river. A fortified settlement on a salt route by the early 12th century, it belonged to the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> and prospered from trade. Its preserved medieval old town is known for brick Gothic churches and for Baumkuchen, the layered 'tree cake'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salzwedel.jpg" alt="Salzwedel" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Björn Gäde at German Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salzwedel_Fachwerk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Salzwedel lies on the river Jeetzel in the north-western part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altmark">Altmark</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, and is the capital of the district of Altmarkkreis Salzwedel. It stands on the German Timber-Frame Road. Test drillings in 1968 revealed a significant reservoir of natural gas near the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The castle of Salzwedel was first documented in 1112. As part of the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> the settlement was first mentioned as a town in 1233, and a new town began to develop to the north-east of the old town in 1247. Salzwedel belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> from 1263 to 1518, and in religious matters to the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a> until 1648. It became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> in 1701, the old and new towns were united in 1713, it joined the Prussian Province of Saxony in 1815 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, and it received a railway connection in 1870.</p><p>Under Nazi rule the town's Jewish residents were systematically deprived of their rights and then expelled. A forced labour camp for some 1,000 women was converted in mid-1944 into a subcamp of the Neuengamme concentration camp, which held over 1,500 female prisoners, mostly Jewish. When the US Army liberated it on 14 April 1945, it found more than ninety women dead of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/typhus">typhus</a>, dysentery and malaria. Five air raids between 1942 and 1945 killed more than 300 people.</p><p>The US Army handed the town to the Soviet Red Army, and it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a>. The border crossing near Salzwedel opened on 9 November 1989, and in 1990 the town received its first democratically elected government. Its official name became Hansestadt Salzwedel on 1 April 2008, recalling its Hanseatic past.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The medieval part of the town remains its commercial and administrative centre. It is enclosed by the historic city wall and town gates, among them the Neuperver Gate, built 1460–1470, the Stonegate of around 1530, the Hungerturm tower and the Kluhs, a storehouse of 1490 built on the wall. The brick Gothic churches of St Marien, St Katharina and St Lorenz stand in the old town, and the birth house of Jenny von Westphalen, later the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a>, is also preserved. Local specialities include Baumkuchen, the layered tree cake, Salzwedel wedding soup and Tiegelbraten, a mutton dish.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Salzwedel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dietrich's harsh rule helped provoke the Great Slav Rising of 983, after which German power collapsed east of the Elbe and the margraves held only the Altmark on the west bank, around <b>Salzwedel</b> and Stendal.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-march">Northern March</a></li></ul></section>
